WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.967 --> 00:00:04.369
That is the textual point of

00:00:04.369 --> 00:00:06.371
view, some unique aspect.

00:00:06.371 --> 00:00:08.940
&gt;&gt; How is working with

00:00:09.207 --> 00:00:10.375
Spielberg different from other

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:10.976
directions?

00:00:11.243 --> 00:00:13.078
&gt;&gt; I work for as many other

00:00:13.345 --> 00:00:13.512
directors.

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:14.379
Many wonderful directors.

00:00:15.947 --> 00:00:17.149
Stephen and I have been

00:00:17.416 --> 00:00:19.084
together for a very long time.

00:00:19.951 --> 00:00:21.219
It's different in the sense

00:00:21.486 --> 00:00:22.954
that we know each other so

00:00:23.221 --> 00:00:23.321
well.

00:00:23.588 --> 00:00:25.957
We have a shorthand of working

00:00:26.391 --> 00:00:28.994
together after all these many

00:00:29.261 --> 00:00:29.361
years.

00:00:29.961 --> 00:00:33.198
He's a close friend, he is many

00:00:33.465 --> 00:00:33.598
things.

00:00:33.932 --> 00:00:37.102
He's a producer and a studio

00:00:37.369 --> 00:00:37.602
and a writer.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:39.204
And a friend.

00:00:40.939 --> 00:00:43.141
He's also not something.

00:00:43.942 --> 00:00:46.978
He's not something you can say

00:00:47.245 --> 00:00:47.345
no to.

00:00:47.946 --> 00:00:50.048
His family and I love him

00:00:50.415 --> 00:00:50.982
dearly.

00:00:51.249 --> 00:00:54.986
And I think he's an

00:00:55.253 --> 00:00:55.454
inspiration.

00:00:55.921 --> 00:00:56.421
Can I museum a?

00:00:57.055 --> 00:00:57.289
I don't know.

00:00:58.924 --> 00:00:59.124
He's amuse.

00:00:59.925 --> 00:01:01.159
He's an inspiration.

00:01:01.426 --> 00:01:03.195
&gt;&gt; Speaking of inspirations,

00:01:03.462 --> 00:01:05.997
you have won five oscars.

00:01:06.431 --> 00:01:07.999
Your nomination for the fable

00:01:08.433 --> 00:01:12.104
mans is your 53rd, it put she

00:01:12.437 --> 00:01:14.005
behind only one man, well

00:01:14.439 --> 00:01:16.441
Disney who had 59 nominations

00:01:16.942 --> 00:01:17.142
for oscars.

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:18.443
What does the recognition from

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:19.111
the oscars mean to you?

00:01:20.979 --> 00:01:23.215
&gt;&gt; Well, it seems very unreal

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:24.449
to me, Chris.

00:01:24.449 --> 00:01:25.117
How could you get 53

00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:25.584
nominations?

00:01:25.984 --> 00:01:26.451
It's impossible.

00:01:26.518 --> 00:01:29.020
No one's live that long to be

00:01:29.287 --> 00:01:29.454
able to --

00:01:29.988 --> 00:01:31.123
you couldn't plan that.

00:01:31.389 --> 00:01:33.091
I never set out to say I have

00:01:33.358 --> 00:01:35.060
all these nominations.

00:01:35.961 --> 00:01:37.095
It boggles my mind.

00:01:37.996 --> 00:01:40.465
I have to credit music and what

00:01:40.465 --> 00:01:43.101
it does for our lives and for

00:01:43.368 --> 00:01:47.072
sustaining our spirit and

00:01:47.339 --> 00:01:48.039
enriching our souls.

00:01:49.007 --> 00:01:51.009
It's like great poetry and

00:01:51.276 --> 00:01:51.476
literature.

00:01:52.043 --> 00:01:54.112
It's something to live for and

00:01:54.479 --> 00:01:55.013
to live by.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.481
&gt;&gt; One of the things I love to

00:01:56.982 --> 00:01:59.151
do is to dig into the process.

00:01:59.417 --> 00:02:04.089
How artists like you create.

00:02:04.489 --> 00:02:07.025
What was your thought about

00:02:07.292 --> 00:02:07.392
jaws?

00:02:08.059 --> 00:02:09.060
What was it you're trying to do

00:02:09.327 --> 00:02:10.128
with a theme from jaws?

00:02:12.063 --> 00:02:13.999
&gt;&gt; In the case of jaws I

00:02:14.499 --> 00:02:17.002
wondered what to do.

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:19.104
How to identify the shock.

00:02:20.005 --> 00:02:23.041
I thought something unstoppable

00:02:23.308 --> 00:02:25.010
like the attack of a shark,

00:02:25.277 --> 00:02:27.012
there is no way you can defend

00:02:27.279 --> 00:02:28.013
yourself from that.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.515
Something is approaching

00:02:30.515 --> 00:02:35.020
inevitably and it's dangerous.

00:02:35.287 --> 00:02:42.994
And even repetitive and low and

00:02:43.261 --> 00:02:43.995
ominous.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:46.531
And start quietly and speed up.

00:02:47.065 --> 00:02:49.067
&gt;&gt; Let's see what you came up

00:02:49.334 --> 00:02:49.501
with here.

00:02:49.768 --> 00:02:50.101
&gt;&gt; All right, good.

00:03:13.291 --> 00:03:14.559
And that is why I didn't go

00:03:14.793 --> 00:03:16.561
into the water for several

00:03:16.695 --> 00:03:18.563
years.

00:03:20.565 --> 00:03:23.435
Spielberg says that score was

00:03:23.702 --> 00:03:26.605
responsible for at least half

00:03:26.871 --> 00:03:27.305
of success.

00:03:27.572 --> 00:03:29.574
&gt;&gt; It might be interesting.

00:03:29.841 --> 00:03:31.443
That clip that you have shown,

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:32.577
how it started slowly in softly

00:03:32.844 --> 00:03:34.713
and how it accelerates as it

00:03:35.614 --> 00:03:36.581
gets closer to this warmer.

00:03:37.282 --> 00:03:39.384
It becomes a greater danger.

00:03:39.651 --> 00:03:40.585
It gets louder and louder.

00:03:41.286 --> 00:03:43.321
A very simple and unstoppable

00:03:43.588 --> 00:03:45.490
rhythmic structure.

00:03:46.591 --> 00:03:50.595
It's relentless and unstoppable

00:03:50.862 --> 00:03:51.696
and maybe something you want to

00:03:51.963 --> 00:03:52.597
get away from.

00:03:53.265 --> 00:03:54.599
&gt;&gt; Definitely something you

00:03:54.666 --> 00:03:55.333
want to get away from.

00:03:56.601 --> 00:03:58.270
Let's go on to the theme from

00:03:58.603 --> 00:03:58.703
star wars.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:03:59.170
Here it is.

00:03:59.437 --> 00:04:14.619
&gt;&gt;

00:04:18.623 --> 00:04:20.625
I've got to say, I saw the

00:04:20.625 --> 00:04:21.459
movie when it came out.

00:04:21.726 --> 00:04:22.627
I never heard anything like

00:04:22.861 --> 00:04:22.961
that.

00:04:23.495 --> 00:04:25.397
What was your thought?

00:04:26.631 --> 00:04:28.633
Of what you want to achieve.

00:04:29.301 --> 00:04:30.635
How do you go about it both in

00:04:30.735 --> 00:04:32.304
terms of the music and how you

00:04:32.637 --> 00:04:32.804
orchestrated?

00:04:33.338 --> 00:04:34.673
&gt;&gt; I worked backwards, Chris.

00:04:35.307 --> 00:04:36.641
This piece is the last piece I

00:04:36.741 --> 00:04:38.643
wrote in the process of writing

00:04:38.643 --> 00:04:39.611
an hour and a half music salon.

00:04:40.312 --> 00:04:42.647
I couldn't seem to get a

00:04:42.647 --> 00:04:47.385
heraldic theme that was --

00:04:47.652 --> 00:04:48.653
that had the heroic aspect that

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:52.657
the orchestra can present in a

00:04:52.657 --> 00:04:54.659
short period of time.

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:58.330
Two hours to play a symphony.

00:04:59.331 --> 00:05:03.368
I finally in desperation really

00:05:03.635 --> 00:05:04.669
really worked out this thing

00:05:04.903 --> 00:05:08.673
with jumps of faith.

00:05:11.743 --> 00:05:12.911
Jumping up to dough again.

00:05:13.678 --> 00:05:16.915
I could show you that it seemed

00:05:17.182 --> 00:05:19.684
to be a direct strong and

00:05:19.951 --> 00:05:22.921
heroic and clear sonorous sound

00:05:23.188 --> 00:05:23.888
from the orchestra.

00:05:24.689 --> 00:05:26.691
And I smile and I hear it.

00:05:26.925 --> 00:05:28.960
Just as an inside thing.

00:05:29.594 --> 00:05:31.730
And it's a particularly

00:05:31.996 --> 00:05:32.697
electrifying brass

00:05:32.897 --> 00:05:35.667
interpretation.

00:05:35.934 --> 00:05:36.835
I don't know how much credit I

00:05:37.102 --> 00:05:37.335
can take that.

00:05:37.635 --> 00:05:40.705
The grace brass section of the

00:05:40.972 --> 00:05:42.774
symphony excited over not me

00:05:43.041 --> 00:05:44.709
conducting it but the arrival

00:05:44.943 --> 00:05:46.811
of the new principal in this

00:05:47.078 --> 00:05:47.746
heraldic phrase.

00:05:48.713 --> 00:05:50.749
&gt;&gt; Then there is the score from

00:05:51.015 --> 00:05:51.116
E.T..

00:05:51.616 --> 00:05:52.717
You say it's one of your

00:05:52.884 --> 00:05:53.651
favorite films.

00:05:54.719 --> 00:05:55.353
Here is the climactic scene.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:21.045
Spielberg said without John

00:06:21.312 --> 00:06:24.215
Williams bikes can't fly.

00:06:24.749 --> 00:06:30.054
&gt;&gt; Well, I love that scene.

00:06:31.022 --> 00:06:36.027
And it always interests me

00:06:36.294 --> 00:06:38.096
wanting to know at least how

00:06:38.363 --> 00:06:39.197
fast you have to go on a

00:06:39.464 --> 00:06:41.232
bicycle to escape philosophy.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.168
To escape gravity.

00:06:44.035 --> 00:06:46.204
What escapes velocity?

00:06:48.006 --> 00:06:50.041
I asked the gentleman from nasa,

00:06:50.308 --> 00:06:53.445
he said 17,500 miles an hour.

00:06:53.978 --> 00:06:57.048
If we can go 17,500 miles an,

00:06:57.315 --> 00:06:58.216
hour the bike will take off and

00:06:58.783 --> 00:07:00.118
we will fly over the moon.

00:07:01.085 --> 00:07:03.221
We buy this, the police were

00:07:03.488 --> 00:07:05.056
chasing the kids on the

00:07:05.323 --> 00:07:05.490
bicycles.

00:07:06.024 --> 00:07:08.059
They reach the speeds and

00:07:08.326 --> 00:07:08.460
flyaway.

00:07:08.793 --> 00:07:09.360
It's wonderful the way we

00:07:09.627 --> 00:07:10.094
accept that.

00:07:10.361 --> 00:07:13.031
It's something about film that

00:07:13.298 --> 00:07:14.299
the magic of what can be done

00:07:15.066 --> 00:07:16.201
with cameras and imagination

00:07:16.468 --> 00:07:18.069
and special effects.

00:07:18.336 --> 00:07:20.038
And yes orchestras are playing

00:07:20.305 --> 00:07:23.041
scenes that have lifted.

00:07:23.308 --> 00:07:26.077
&gt;&gt; Let's talk about the

00:07:26.344 --> 00:07:28.112
difference that music makes to

00:07:28.379 --> 00:07:28.813
a movie.

00:07:29.013 --> 00:07:32.183
Here is the classic theme from

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:33.585
raiders of the lost ark.

00:07:33.852 --> 00:07:48.867
&gt;&gt; Spielberg says that the

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:49.267
two

00:07:49.534 --> 00:07:52.237
of you had a performance and an

00:07:52.504 --> 00:07:54.305
appearance in a concert where

00:07:54.839 --> 00:07:55.406
you played a scene from one of

00:07:55.673 --> 00:07:57.175
the raiders movies without any

00:07:57.442 --> 00:07:59.377
music and that frankly he says

00:07:59.644 --> 00:08:01.312
it was wrong and boring.

00:08:01.579 --> 00:08:03.214
And then you played it with the

00:08:03.481 --> 00:08:05.216
music, you played it with the

00:08:05.483 --> 00:08:06.217
orchestra.

00:08:06.484 --> 00:08:08.186
It was a totally different

00:08:08.453 --> 00:08:08.853
experience.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:10.221
&gt;&gt; It is completely different.

00:08:10.488 --> 00:08:12.857
It will contract and compress

00:08:12.857 --> 00:08:13.224
the time certainly.

00:08:13.491 --> 00:08:14.325
It's quick music.

00:08:14.859 --> 00:08:17.395
It seemed like the one you

00:08:17.662 --> 00:08:19.464
refer to is about four minutes.

00:08:20.198 --> 00:08:21.533
Running at without the music,

00:08:21.799 --> 00:08:23.434
it seems like six minutes.

00:08:23.701 --> 00:08:26.337
It's almost like riding a

00:08:26.871 --> 00:08:27.305
horse.

00:08:27.305 --> 00:08:28.206
You don't feel it.

00:08:30.174 --> 00:08:30.542
If you're fighting the

